<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baton Rouge</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baton Rouge is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> and the capital of the US state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>. A French post in the 18th century, it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1763 as part of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1779. In 1810 American settlers seized its Spanish fort and declared the Republic of West Florida, soon annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baton-rouge.jpg" alt="Baton Rouge" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Spatms · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Baton_Rouge,_Louisiana_from_Tiger_Stadium_(LSU).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Baton Rouge stands on the eastern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida-parishes">Florida Parishes</a> region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>. Its historical importance rests on the Istrouma Bluff, the first natural bluff upriver from the Mississippi River Delta at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. The bluff allowed a business quarter to develop safe from seasonal flooding, and a levee system was later built from it southward to protect the riverfront and the low-lying farmland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human habitation in the area has been dated to between 12000 and 6500 BCE, from evidence found along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, Comite and Amite rivers. Hunter-gatherer societies built earthwork mounds from roughly the fourth millennium BCE. In 1698 the French explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville</a> led a party up the Mississippi and saw a red pole marking the boundary between the hunting grounds of the Houma and the Bayagoula. The French name le bâton rouge, 'the red stick', translates a native term rendered as Istrouma, possibly a corruption of the Choctaw iti humma, 'red pole'.</p><p>Baton Rouge was incorporated in 1817, and the Pentagon Barracks were completed in 1822. The site had been used by Spanish, French and British authorities, was part of the short-lived Republic of West Florida, and was later occupied by both the Confederate States Army and the United States Army. The city has therefore been ruled by six different nations: the French, Spanish and British in the colonial era, the Republic of West Florida, the United States, the Confederate States of America, and the United States again after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. The state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> took ownership of the barracks in 1951, and the complex was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1976.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Baton Rouge is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> and the seat of East Baton Rouge Parish, with which its government has been consolidated since 1947. It had 227,470 inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and its metropolitan area had 870,569. It is the home of Louisiana State University, the state's largest university, and of Southern University, the flagship of the only historically black university system in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Its port is the farthest upstream on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> that can handle Panamax ships, and the city is a centre of the petrochemical industry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baton Rouge, Louisiana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>About 311 Muscogee being forcibly removed to the Indian Territory died when the steamboat Monmouth collided at night on the Mississippi River above <b>Baton Rouge</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-31">31 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers came from the older colonies and Britain to farm along the Mississippi near Natchez and <b>Baton Rouge</b>, while Pensacola and Mobile lived on the deerskin trade with the Muscogee Confederacy, Choctaw Nation and Chickasaw Nation.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-west-florida">Province of West Florida</a></li><li><blockquote>Many settlers in the <b>Baton Rouge</b> district were Americans.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-west-florida">Republic of West Florida</a></li></ul></section>
